      "narrative": "FC Zandmeer's revenue of €129.0m is 3.5 times the league's typical club, and it shows in the table: first place on the largest wage bill. Costs have caught up with income, though. Total costs of €130.1m left an operating loss of €1.1m, the first in five years, and it took €19.4m of profit on player sales, including K. Vos's €13.6m move to a league rival, to produce a net profit of €13.2m.\n\nThe balance sheet is strong. Net assets are €200.5m, debt of €25.0m is small against them, and current assets cover current liabilities comfortably. The distress score is zero.\n\nChampions League football looks likely, and our sample projection of a 25% revenue boost would bring wages down to around 45% of revenue. The regulatory side is less comfortable: wages plus player amortisation already equal 76% of revenue, above UEFA's 70% squad-cost limit, a breach the club would have to address. With €21.4m of net transfer spend over three years and the operating result falling every year since 2021, rising costs are eroding a very strong position.",
