      "narrative": "SC Duinwijk's wage bill has grown much faster than its income. Wages of €19.3m now take 84% of revenue of €23.1m, up from 72% in 2020, while revenue itself has barely moved. Total costs of €28.8m left an operating loss of €5.7m, the fifth operating loss in a row and the largest of them.\n\nWe couldn't read the club's 2024 net result reliably from its published accounts, so it isn't shown here, and nor is a distress score. The operating loss and the balance sheet tell a consistent story, though: net assets have fallen from €12.0m in 2020 to €5.2m, debt has risen from €10.1m to €14.3m, and current assets only just cover current liabilities. Cash is €2.4m.\n\nFourth place on the third-largest wage bill is roughly what the club pays for. With €3.2m of net transfer spend over three years and little income from player sales, the club is not funding itself through the market the way the league's top two do. On last year's trend, net assets would run out within about two years unless wages come down.",
